In article <199401202123.NAA00217@well.sf.ca.us>,
Brian D Williams <talon57@well.sf.ca.us> writes:
> The recent earthquake and its results prompt me to ask a simple
>question; wouldn't it make more sense in earthquake country to live
>in a geodesic dome? An average geodesic can shrug off an earthquake
>not to mention 200+ MPH winds (tornado alley residents) and in the
>event of a catastrophic failure you'll have 99% less material
>falling on you.

Sensible for living in, yes.  But, remember that you will probably want to
sell it some day.  I love domes; they are superior to boxes for a variety
of reasons.  I would have bought or constructed one a long time ago, if it
were not for the disappointing fact that they have such an awful resale
value in comparison to a conventional structure of the same square footage
and age.  I learned the hard way that even a slightly unconventional home
is exceedingly difficult to sell, even if every other point is excellent or
superior.  I had a robust, nicely appointed two-story A-frame chalet,
similar to what you see at high elevations where there is often a huge load
of snow.  However, it was in a lush coastal valley where it never snowed.
The fact that it could probably withstand an 8.0 quake with nary a crack
did not impress most potential buyers, even given that it was less than ten
miles away from an active stretch of the San Andreas fault.  You have to
just hope that a buyer with similar taste and concern as you happens along.

