Subject: [94-1-356] Imposing Structures from Within and Without (was Extropian marriage)

Kennita asks:
>>>Might there be some advantage to Extropians being married according to the
>>>government?  For example, if one partner "dies" (also according to the
>>>government), might legally-sanctioned marriage help the remaining partner
>>>to protect his/her estate from money-grubbing relatives who try to grab it
>>
>>No. Why would I volunteer something very special to me (my relationship)
>>to be in a contract with the government? My perspective is that the whole
>>thing becomes tainted with such a involvement.

>Taint is not my concern at this point.  If my next ten-thousand-plus years
>are at risk because my relatives may renege on some contract they signed
>and the government might decide to give them everything I own and have me
>buried or cremated instead of cryonically suspended because I have no
>spouse or children to lay claim on my estate or to stand up for my rights,

Things aren't as bad as this suggests.  Most cryonicists arrange their
funding through life insurance.  The Sum Assured is paid out to the
beneficiary, i.e. the cryonics company, and so is never part of the
estate and cannot be contested by relatives.

In the cryonics contract you have to specify where the money goes if,
for some reason, the suspension cannot or does not go ahead.  For
instance, it might all be returned to relatives, a fraction might be
donated for cryonics research or general funds, or the whole lot might go
to research.  Whatever the allocation, relatives can be told that it's
all earmarked for research in the event of burial / cremation.

This is no solution for those who are too old or sick to obtain life
insurance.  And there's always the risk of non-cooperation by relatives
-- you can't be sure what they'd do.  To paraphrase from a previous post:
"We were only humouring hir, we don't really believe that cryonics tripe
could ever work."  But at least, there is the prospect of relatives being
powerless to engage in money-grubbing, or to prevent a suspension due to
lack of finance.

