>capacity" , like for example your hard disk has.

Associative memories *do* fill up.  The end result is at best forgetting 
old material or at worst degrading all material somehow.  Kanerva's 
monograph Sparse Distributed Memory describes an associative memory 
and discusses the problem.  Neural networks also have computable memory
limits.

In theory, there is a limit to memory in any physical system, but there
isn't any good way to test this for humans--we have high bandwidth input, 
but output is not nearly as high.  Perhaps some kind of nanotech disassembler
could measure entropy of the brain...


