Mark Plus:

>Unfortunately, I fear a great many Extropians will continue to
>confuse convenience with necessity,

'Twould help us avoid this confusion if you provided us with a
standard by which to distinguish the two.

I think the fundamental problem with your analysis is that you
underrate gradual improvements.  Think of how interest compounds.
You don't ever get a huge return at once, but it builds and
builds gradually into quite something.  We didn't get the highest
standard of living in history all at once, but through a gradual
process of capital accumulation, investment, etc.
	
I've already criticized your analysis of Reaganomics as pro-saving,
which it wasn't.  It was pro-investment, not pro-saving.  There's
a big difference.

>	However, this productivity revolution has failed by its
>success.  With growing efficiency in making and moving things,
>fewer and fewer workers are required in manufacturing.  A few
>decades ago manufacturing workers were the majority in the
>workforce of the U.S. and other developed countries.  Today in
>the U.S. less than 15% of the workforce works in manufacturing. 
>Farm workers account for perhaps another 4% or less (they being
>the other productivity success story), so the remaining 80+
>percent of us are perforce knowledge workers or service workers. 

I don't think the decline in the number of people employed in
agriculture and manufacturing in the USA is a function of the
increased productivity of such workers.  Rather, I think that
employers have faced rising costs of employing human laborers -
largely regulatory costs and government mandates - and that
in response they have replaced as many US workers as possible
with capital goods and less-costly, third-world foreign labor.

I also think the decline of economic growth, productivity, and
wages is due to regulatory and taxation costs, but I'll have to
do some more research in order to meet your challenge.  BTW, I
respect your effort.  It takes a lot to compose the sort of
essay you've undertaken and to submit it to such a bunch of tough
critics.

>Drucker warns on p. 82 that the
>productivity of knowledge workers and service workers, "rather
>than the productivity of the people who make and move things, is
>_the_ productivity of a developed economy.  It is abysmally low. 
>The productivity of people doing knowledge work and service work
>may actually be going down rather than going up."   

I suspect that the difficulties in measuring the productivity of
such workers may be hiding the productivity gains involved.

>	Here is where I part company with Drucker.  I suppose the
>kinds of knowledge work involving data processing could be made
>more productive, but the productivity of service work, which
>employs a greater percentage of the workforce, faces (a) human
>motor limits, and (b) limits from the nature of human social
>interaction.  

Human motor limits can be overcome with automation, as has already
happened to a great degree in manufacturing, and human labor can be
reallocated to managing/servicing the automation.

>	And regarding (b), a great deal of service work, as in
>department stores and restaurants, involves social interaction; 
>this can't be time-and-motioned studied into greater productivity
>without alienating the public.  Retail clerks and waitresses now
>outnumber manufacturing workers in the U.S.  The productivity of
>the former two categories of workers hasn't gone up in decades,
>and probably won't go up in decades to come.

That seems silly to me.  Here's a simple way to improve the produc-
tivity of waitresses: computerize and network restaurant menus so
that orders can be issued by patrons from their tables automatically,
somewhat like they can program jukeboxes at some eateries.  Combine
this with card-reader billing as in grocery stores.  Then all
waitresses have to do is transport food.  I thought of this years
ago, and have no idea why it hasn't been done yet except that
perhaps restauranteurs aren't very computer-literate.

Our videotape rental company already has similar things: when I
phoned it up to order a couple of videos the other night, all their
operators were busy, so my order was taken by a voice-mail system
and confirmed by a callback from the next available clerk.  That
way I didn't get stuck on hold.

And I'm no expert on these sort of things.  I expect those that are
will be able to come up with all sorts of improvements I can't
possibly imagine.

